<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Säckingen Abbey</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Säckingen Abbey was a house of noble canonesses on the Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> at Säckingen, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, traditionally founded by the Irish missionary Saint Fridolin in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. It held extensive lands, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linth">Linth</a> valley of Glarus, whose administration passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> as its advocates. The Glarners bought out its rights in 1395, and the abbey was dissolved in 1806.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to the life of Saint Fridolin, Saint Hilarius appeared to him in a dream and told him to go to an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in the lands of the Alamanni. Clovis granted him the island, and Fridolin travelled by way of Strasburg and Coire, founding churches in honour of Hilarius. The people on the banks, who grazed their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> on the island of Säckingen, took him for a cattle thief and drove him off, but he was allowed back on showing Clovis's deed of gift, and founded a church and monastery there.</p><p>Of this story, only Fridolin's identity as an Irish missionary who preached in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and founded a monastery on the island is regarded as historically tenable, and the dates are unknown. The abbey is placed in the 6th or 7th century. It had both monks and nuns, but only the convent of women grew into an important religious, economic and cultural institution of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Little is known of the abbey before the 9th century. On 10 February 878 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a> gave Säckingen, with the monasteries of St. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-and-regula">Felix and Regula</a> in Zurich, to his wife Richardis as a royal estate. The grant carried wide political rights and large lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and Frick valleys, the southern Hotzenwald, along Lake Walen and in the valley of Glarus.</p><p>In 1173 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> granted the imperial bailiwick of the abbey to Count Albert III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a>, the basis of later Habsburg sovereignty over Säckingen. In 1307 the abbess was raised to the rank of Reichsfürst, Imperial Prince. When the Glarus valley broke away in 1395, it kept the figure of Saint Fridolin on its coat of arms. The buildings were renovated and extended between 1565 and 1575. With the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> in 1806, the abbey was closed and its property passed, with the town of Säckingen, to the Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Säckingen Abbey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Linth valley belonged to the abbey of <b>Säckingen</b>, whose rights passed to the Habsburgs.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-glarus">Canton of Glarus</a></li></ul></section>
